Arm architectural instructions
------------------------------
The decoder and encoder are implemented from the publicly documented Arm
Architecture Reference Manual. During development, output is also compared with
independent tools and data, including LLVM, GNU binutils, and Vector 35's
`arch-arm64` test corpus. Those comparison inputs are local development
references and are excluded from the published crate.

The `arch-arm64` project identifies its test material as:

    arch-arm64
    Copyright 2020-2024 Vector 35 Inc.
    Apache License, Version 2.0
    https://github.com/Vector35/arch-arm64

Apple implementation-defined instructions
-----------------------------------------
fARM64 includes separately runtime-gated support for Apple implementation-
defined instruction families. Apple AMX operation names and encodings reference
the public `corsix/amx` reverse-engineering project:

    https://github.com/corsix/amx

Apple GXF (`GENTER`/`GEXIT`) encodings reference Asahi Linux's public Apple
Proprietary Instructions documentation; background on GXF is provided by Sven
Peter's “Apple Silicon Hardware Secrets: SPRR and Guarded Exception Levels
(GXF)”:

    https://asahilinux.org/docs/hw/cpu/apple-instructions/
    https://blog.svenpeter.dev/posts/m1_sprr_gxf/

These families are not Arm architectural extensions and are disabled by
`FeatureSet::BASE`; callers must explicitly
enable `Feature::AppleAmx` or `Feature::Gxf` (or use `FeatureSet::ALL`). Their
encodings are pinned by focused decode, encode, gating, and reserved-form tests.
